[{:db/id "q-274841/field-1/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" :answer/id #uuid "c8015c35-1438-5f46-bce0-c045ff46ab28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274841/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08" :answer/id #uuid "6e320f97-d10d-590e-9abf-832310f813da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274841/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb897ae-ad55-5d50-9379-b17eb38cebf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274841/field-1/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" :answer-field/choices ["q-274841/field-1/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" "q-274841/field-1/ccc5b7f3cfdb4c2dfc0e1f31be54559a1ab3adaf142e500fc1f1b7bc87e93b08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274841/field-2/7e35823d10f65dabc95929142749aca3c62d1af7b6ab33b0ab3042beceb457c5" :answer/id #uuid "8cbfd87e-987c-5d00-8471-29324e914989" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274841/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd9de2b-4327-5617-957b-92d2fc5d7a73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274841/field-2/7e35823d10f65dabc95929142749aca3c62d1af7b6ab33b0ab3042beceb457c5" :answer-field/choices ["q-274841/field-2/7e35823d10f65dabc95929142749aca3c62d1af7b6ab33b0ab3042beceb457c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274841" :question/id #uuid "efe0ceba-4ecd-5baa-8135-9d9e9b625fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-274841" :question/problem "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order**, the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-274841/field-1" "q-274841/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-274841"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-29186/selection/eb7bc2fe49dc2392f1cf3683effc5ca0e8ae6c38c6fa73641e98096fa319a1fb" :answer/id #uuid "b3cfef6c-47ee-56a9-9a51-7b6f3cfdca1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29186/selection/ee38d41860bf4acf8e22202aac49e343bc1784b70af419966459a77ba4ae705d" :answer/id #uuid "e219e4a4-1e5c-55a3-a0cc-34e1060f4943" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29186/selection/cf0763f4eb93dc5c80bf8f79dc04c2cc36678d63b9db728ab4d9530ec9561e8e" :answer/id #uuid "ce3c28fc-5644-5a60-99ce-980fcae5f06d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29186/selection/a4415149f8556702e510bddb1e2e02cd394ea40d49fab618c2923f3f0c06682b" :answer/id #uuid "4d038bad-45af-5d5a-a8ac-caf8ac71f485" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,700" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29186/selection/2e8bf359258fd334c4c585e5a7bc6f95e6f781c691160ea6e355ea7b7ac9cd14" :answer/id #uuid "af905be5-8c25-5e8f-8457-c1bc4a2b8c5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29186/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e15013d9-d50f-5fb4-ac96-e05f0a0c5edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-29186/selection/ee38d41860bf4acf8e22202aac49e343bc1784b70af419966459a77ba4ae705d" :answer-field/choices ["q-29186/selection/eb7bc2fe49dc2392f1cf3683effc5ca0e8ae6c38c6fa73641e98096fa319a1fb" "q-29186/selection/ee38d41860bf4acf8e22202aac49e343bc1784b70af419966459a77ba4ae705d" "q-29186/selection/cf0763f4eb93dc5c80bf8f79dc04c2cc36678d63b9db728ab4d9530ec9561e8e" "q-29186/selection/a4415149f8556702e510bddb1e2e02cd394ea40d49fab618c2923f3f0c06682b" "q-29186/selection/2e8bf359258fd334c4c585e5a7bc6f95e6f781c691160ea6e355ea7b7ac9cd14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-29186" :question/id #uuid "904087af-f67c-5a68-9781-ad06a94be972" :question/math-academy-id "q-29186" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x={120}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot 240=28\\,800.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-29186/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-29186"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-28902/selection/104d1e0f57901066548a7bb71acc3e45fb1e67442fc888335c2ad54d838ee95f" :answer/id #uuid "b2afbe3f-808e-5f9d-8827-01707cd9ce1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28902/selection/ed5b0237607f5fd14ef0ef6a03ad488eb352c18840ce7bca6c5e90c8402e7c2e" :answer/id #uuid "43a6f777-6799-57d4-a5e2-27b8859480b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28902/selection/b627bdd0369562afa8f12be8f167039ff5f7a2bdc550b832dce7f5205e0a89bc" :answer/id #uuid "fae0e687-191c-5bd0-bcdc-596055ff5915" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28902/selection/166cfdde53426fc934fcc0274e967ae6bf4d85028935fbd99859a43c0ebf8d49" :answer/id #uuid "ce62c64e-ac42-58c7-af6d-6b19c931f3ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28902/selection/080aa7aa965688cd2badb7b57dce39c6db33b60e55a0036b313d953319d0b4d6" :answer/id #uuid "c9a2508d-1cfe-5057-954d-a4d15f0271ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28902/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "02ad47a4-ad1c-5dd4-b55d-a462f6796776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-28902/selection/080aa7aa965688cd2badb7b57dce39c6db33b60e55a0036b313d953319d0b4d6" :answer-field/choices ["q-28902/selection/104d1e0f57901066548a7bb71acc3e45fb1e67442fc888335c2ad54d838ee95f" "q-28902/selection/ed5b0237607f5fd14ef0ef6a03ad488eb352c18840ce7bca6c5e90c8402e7c2e" "q-28902/selection/b627bdd0369562afa8f12be8f167039ff5f7a2bdc550b832dce7f5205e0a89bc" "q-28902/selection/166cfdde53426fc934fcc0274e967ae6bf4d85028935fbd99859a43c0ebf8d49" "q-28902/selection/080aa7aa965688cd2badb7b57dce39c6db33b60e55a0036b313d953319d0b4d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-28902" :question/id #uuid "8e6aa2e6-3374-55d1-b3c7-5bb059323dce" :question/math-academy-id "q-28902" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=3$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-28902/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-28902"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-28993/selection/f0888baa062181c2a231a28e40c7f11c053b4ec3bbec727c34510aa1a3f6bc7a" :answer/id #uuid "d498d86c-e081-522b-89c3-fe045c2d2f5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28993/selection/e55580949058f9edb60d8b72a4febfa5a129758d0c6911f8b36ebb8f65cc12b5" :answer/id #uuid "e181af48-a576-534e-9864-8db8815d8a54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "34\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28993/selection/ee38d41860bf4acf8e22202aac49e343bc1784b70af419966459a77ba4ae705d" :answer/id #uuid "4ae971ba-a269-5222-9a85-88dad8df0e15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28993/selection/56aa5ec6f972b749f2f5258741a049b6681dc2c06bc4245d9d9e7f9af7d90117" :answer/id #uuid "50aa02dd-8989-56a8-bba1-436590b4b0ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28993/selection/20e0d2469bdd0644450befb8129e550388ea9ca86c53217214c8633741f2211f" :answer/id #uuid "864aea57-b86b-5fa1-b79b-2bcab7884d28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32\\,400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-28993/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1093e2e6-7722-5568-8757-583bc7d345a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-28993/selection/56aa5ec6f972b749f2f5258741a049b6681dc2c06bc4245d9d9e7f9af7d90117" :answer-field/choices ["q-28993/selection/f0888baa062181c2a231a28e40c7f11c053b4ec3bbec727c34510aa1a3f6bc7a" "q-28993/selection/e55580949058f9edb60d8b72a4febfa5a129758d0c6911f8b36ebb8f65cc12b5" "q-28993/selection/ee38d41860bf4acf8e22202aac49e343bc1784b70af419966459a77ba4ae705d" "q-28993/selection/56aa5ec6f972b749f2f5258741a049b6681dc2c06bc4245d9d9e7f9af7d90117" "q-28993/selection/20e0d2469bdd0644450befb8129e550388ea9ca86c53217214c8633741f2211f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-28993" :question/id #uuid "05c4151f-8bb2-5b70-8fe7-236802b47fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-28993" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={45}^{∘},{315}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot 315=14\\,175.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-28993/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-28993"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76488/selection/e55580949058f9edb60d8b72a4febfa5a129758d0c6911f8b36ebb8f65cc12b5" :answer/id #uuid "9db49764-06d5-534d-b50f-55592d0d83ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "34\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76488/selection/ee38d41860bf4acf8e22202aac49e343bc1784b70af419966459a77ba4ae705d" :answer/id #uuid "566a7d4b-e77e-55ad-83a8-44129c098e1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28\\,800" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76488/selection/f0888baa062181c2a231a28e40c7f11c053b4ec3bbec727c34510aa1a3f6bc7a" :answer/id #uuid "f541561c-b685-514a-8586-3c8f378c37f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "31\\,500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76488/selection/9454e9219837e5f945dbebfd40d132d63478ad31e323bd2e727357a3fd3c0691" :answer/id #uuid "522e70ee-798b-5b0d-8704-08d43193ebb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,150" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76488/selection/83571eb82b4127271b1e2addbddab517c93e99c9300074c00dd941e05abafa15" :answer/id #uuid "f5b104c4-d1ef-5d68-ad94-4e6b8106760e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76488/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e61d4e65-ad94-5bbf-8c32-f580e2a746bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76488/selection/f0888baa062181c2a231a28e40c7f11c053b4ec3bbec727c34510aa1a3f6bc7a" :answer-field/choices ["q-76488/selection/e55580949058f9edb60d8b72a4febfa5a129758d0c6911f8b36ebb8f65cc12b5" "q-76488/selection/ee38d41860bf4acf8e22202aac49e343bc1784b70af419966459a77ba4ae705d" "q-76488/selection/f0888baa062181c2a231a28e40c7f11c053b4ec3bbec727c34510aa1a3f6bc7a" "q-76488/selection/9454e9219837e5f945dbebfd40d132d63478ad31e323bd2e727357a3fd3c0691" "q-76488/selection/83571eb82b4127271b1e2addbddab517c93e99c9300074c00dd941e05abafa15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76488" :question/id #uuid "e382d847-b095-5055-a3a9-70bf6b257ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-76488" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={150}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${150}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={150}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/d61c6dbd94be14f2a455791a584a00f19ab7b52452b3acda984c5b278a5e39ed.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={150}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=150\\cdot 210=31\\,500.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-76488/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3e2ddd-4937-5837-a03a-b7568bff9b87"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76488"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120456/selection/8fac037b426311ee219887f482807cbb46c0f9e476dfc3501c13028fead5ce61" :answer/id #uuid "05d3cc85-6bb9-54a3-aaaa-448a64b11ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120456/selection/f3ce197c0bf0ff8b852cf7f6b09aed316591849f14268aca6e2ceb70c79bfb8a" :answer/id #uuid "c4b6a924-ad2f-5164-b6e4-a8598dce1d9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120456/selection/70738cb9b4d257dda8a72ea605b6358220661f35610122861e861f1b8f6badb1" :answer/id #uuid "418c406c-b628-54c7-bd70-2c29457b412b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120456/selection/65cca031fb0c3d58e669bcbabbe43f8d54e4350ed6e256d1f00fa76238fc83d6" :answer/id #uuid "09109bdc-22cb-5df6-9abe-b92b3bf86d8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120456/selection/1401f6a7f62484b8002216c84667903d23e45d131549d09d34471b740be2200d" :answer/id #uuid "2a615638-b235-5dbb-9f57-dcff1d743fe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120456/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "63758ecc-6ec3-5da6-85d9-c4a815a4e3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120456/selection/8fac037b426311ee219887f482807cbb46c0f9e476dfc3501c13028fead5ce61" :answer-field/choices ["q-120456/selection/8fac037b426311ee219887f482807cbb46c0f9e476dfc3501c13028fead5ce61" "q-120456/selection/f3ce197c0bf0ff8b852cf7f6b09aed316591849f14268aca6e2ceb70c79bfb8a" "q-120456/selection/70738cb9b4d257dda8a72ea605b6358220661f35610122861e861f1b8f6badb1" "q-120456/selection/65cca031fb0c3d58e669bcbabbe43f8d54e4350ed6e256d1f00fa76238fc83d6" "q-120456/selection/1401f6a7f62484b8002216c84667903d23e45d131549d09d34471b740be2200d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120456" :question/id #uuid "9fb32491-b445-5fcb-aa6f-323a9d2a0ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120456" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5}=-\\sqrt{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5} & =-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\sqrt{5})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})=2.034\\,443…≈2.034\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.034\\,4$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.034\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/f659a13a5a4c9ae998c33b398b777decc8a5603fd18b5579ddebc897ea3dafca.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.107\\,2 \\\\  & =4.248\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.034\\,4\\cdot 4.248\\,7≈8.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120456/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120456"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-324392/field-1/441f9a41e4c1d7c29719a73a7d795c394ee1337b1a990ebfb59a7b69a254418f" :answer/id #uuid "b6022a7a-f852-55bf-bb4b-9acf8b8512df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-324392/field-1/57f27b4de16f64fcab2f3b810a7087fb3b270a063a6807c410b738013ce41fde" :answer/id #uuid "36f4ba3d-e1d9-5916-84c5-d4d4a76b5209" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-324392/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "165f6e76-65f1-5666-bafa-ac1e4c15bc48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-324392/field-1/441f9a41e4c1d7c29719a73a7d795c394ee1337b1a990ebfb59a7b69a254418f" :answer-field/choices ["q-324392/field-1/441f9a41e4c1d7c29719a73a7d795c394ee1337b1a990ebfb59a7b69a254418f" "q-324392/field-1/57f27b4de16f64fcab2f3b810a7087fb3b270a063a6807c410b738013ce41fde"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-324392/field-2/5d82d3405d35cad3389a01eccd9c1e95c5305a27c9d9c2505cc5ae44484e4a24" :answer/id #uuid "01482d2c-d5c2-54d7-932b-1cd30760092f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-324392/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "16d1a995-a765-56e3-837a-7594d151b089" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-324392/field-2/5d82d3405d35cad3389a01eccd9c1e95c5305a27c9d9c2505cc5ae44484e4a24" :answer-field/choices ["q-324392/field-2/5d82d3405d35cad3389a01eccd9c1e95c5305a27c9d9c2505cc5ae44484e4a24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-324392" :question/id #uuid "d736f3dc-378b-533b-985b-098a4888328b" :question/math-academy-id "q-324392" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{4}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{3}{4})≈0.723\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/3b95f4a3d92d2214472f8c72f9322f6d1aa89a6e2c4f8cc2ae9f13015217a84d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.723 \\\\  & ≈5.560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=0.7$ and $x=5.6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-324392/field-1" "q-324392/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-324392"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-324401/field-1/f34afcd1d8d06e16f767da9737fea297f9b48d4af4bb29ac0a317e64a3a131e9" :answer/id #uuid "0379519a-f193-50a9-868b-84dd5b4253d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-324401/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "591fbe4f-aafd-5d81-bbe7-8f0c605581f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-324401/field-1/f34afcd1d8d06e16f767da9737fea297f9b48d4af4bb29ac0a317e64a3a131e9" :answer-field/choices ["q-324401/field-1/f34afcd1d8d06e16f767da9737fea297f9b48d4af4bb29ac0a317e64a3a131e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-324401/field-2/12421a6e1ec720412f010275bbc108feb3faf4606c642b7e436f211edc66b521" :answer/id #uuid "8983d381-2a60-5879-b957-a3ac8f7ef730" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-324401/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6a37fc8-abfa-5d16-a531-194386fd17fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-324401/field-2/12421a6e1ec720412f010275bbc108feb3faf4606c642b7e436f211edc66b521" :answer-field/choices ["q-324401/field-2/12421a6e1ec720412f010275bbc108feb3faf4606c642b7e436f211edc66b521"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-324401" :question/id #uuid "aca43237-2858-5ef6-b0b1-f24a1198a232" :question/math-academy-id "q-324401" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})≈2.062\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈\\pi -2.062≈1.080.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.080.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c8020c19d17f3e79b825cb4e0cad4471e68ec0e2618a12578115a0fc8c014599.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi +1.080 \\\\  & ≈4.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=2.1$ and $x=4.2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-324401/field-1" "q-324401/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-324401"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-324397/field-1/80e5c4244ec21efa1c34bdbfe08fc135024fa21b1d8b8e83e3a0eb8925976736" :answer/id #uuid "4671674d-d300-5dac-8d2a-6c7134757439" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "115.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-324397/field-1/6da21dfa3bc94046bd215d7d47e26c032faea5e3cccc22b9cecb13a2e6188e95" :answer/id #uuid "616c1241-11ea-5aee-8dd0-0fdba6656d2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "115.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-324397/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cac192cd-5660-5cfc-995b-620c521d75d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-324397/field-1/80e5c4244ec21efa1c34bdbfe08fc135024fa21b1d8b8e83e3a0eb8925976736" :answer-field/choices ["q-324397/field-1/80e5c4244ec21efa1c34bdbfe08fc135024fa21b1d8b8e83e3a0eb8925976736" "q-324397/field-1/6da21dfa3bc94046bd215d7d47e26c032faea5e3cccc22b9cecb13a2e6188e95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-324397/field-2/dfddba49ff0d1e9e420be9e94b195819774489660a18043284473f42d3f0542e" :answer/id #uuid "1a8a757b-3fc2-5e6a-9e31-629d52bf1e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "244.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-324397/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "aec00fe1-72ae-5414-ba1f-56781b77c547" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-324397/field-2/dfddba49ff0d1e9e420be9e94b195819774489660a18043284473f42d3f0542e" :answer-field/choices ["q-324397/field-2/dfddba49ff0d1e9e420be9e94b195819774489660a18043284473f42d3f0542e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-324397" :question/id #uuid "eb05d369-7730-510a-afde-7896782f6715" :question/math-academy-id "q-324397" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.3$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2.3})≈{115.771}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{180}^{∘}-{115.771}^{∘}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/974fad6534267df035b759df9064ed7dfaff80cb299c41744e8888f248ed1eee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{180}^{∘}+{64.229}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{244.229}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=115.8\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=244.2\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-324397/field-1" "q-324397/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-324397"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-30468/selection/dc8876f3bf74ae5b0296fef87f5627834c52d090a4593ed8b40f8cc7897bd239" :answer/id #uuid "062d4e2d-c8ad-5cdd-8c0c-9eec97d6b1c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19\\,876" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30468/selection/1ba85f7a56a9624cceeea8a722dc9f17b8dd74cf4533e49cc43673e0b606889d" :answer/id #uuid "367fe7ab-1252-5bf3-8468-e7b19844d621" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,653" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30468/selection/ae841e8075409c5ffc6ab349765503c23b230cc3dfdcbaa182e9faf897581d1f" :answer/id #uuid "a1171f0f-04f7-593e-9435-ca31eedf1ffe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23\\,642" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30468/selection/5ebbe3297f4195ff568c708f673de75df7a7643fbea91d12cbbe92582741b4a8" :answer/id #uuid "78736c4f-1f53-5d5c-93de-0aef30ded33c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27\\,128" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30468/selection/e268050f72954223ecf8c4ac9053378a82df326acf368cece1a8372162dd4e4e" :answer/id #uuid "1f22aaed-940f-5764-9657-0035c0f5e9ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,894" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-30468/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0ee720a-4bc4-56fe-975d-e6705c7bb88e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-30468/selection/ae841e8075409c5ffc6ab349765503c23b230cc3dfdcbaa182e9faf897581d1f" :answer-field/choices ["q-30468/selection/dc8876f3bf74ae5b0296fef87f5627834c52d090a4593ed8b40f8cc7897bd239" "q-30468/selection/1ba85f7a56a9624cceeea8a722dc9f17b8dd74cf4533e49cc43673e0b606889d" "q-30468/selection/ae841e8075409c5ffc6ab349765503c23b230cc3dfdcbaa182e9faf897581d1f" "q-30468/selection/5ebbe3297f4195ff568c708f673de75df7a7643fbea91d12cbbe92582741b4a8" "q-30468/selection/e268050f72954223ecf8c4ac9053378a82df326acf368cece1a8372162dd4e4e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-30468" :question/id #uuid "285d836c-d6b2-57e5-9c9a-74f208e12b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-30468" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4)=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4) & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-16 & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=86.416\\,678{…}^{∘}≈{86.417}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${86.417}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/15e99d348206191535a2b6fb9d85893e666abd16f5f9fc61fdf4e19424e2fde9.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{86.417}^{∘} \\\\  & ={273.583}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=86.417\\cdot 273.583≈23\\,642,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-30468/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "387e57ad-6f80-5b90-8adf-7bf05618b532"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-30468"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-29177"] :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},-{60}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=60\\cdot (-60)=-3\\,600.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-29182/selection/83571eb82b4127271b1e2addbddab517c93e99c9300074c00dd941e05abafa15" :answer/id #uuid "5d744648-c075-59af-a076-09a1fa55b101" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,900" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29182/selection/56aa5ec6f972b749f2f5258741a049b6681dc2c06bc4245d9d9e7f9af7d90117" :answer/id #uuid "6c75fafa-3b73-5504-bb1b-47983aa24dad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\,175" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29182/selection/1b07ec847b0ad69ffaa1f16e85ab83d4a29995c786c7293f0c7f63ccc7740310" :answer/id #uuid "850c98a8-4c44-569f-9779-19801333aaf2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29182/selection/9bf898dca7fc0a32413abe9b644d58c4852132a28ce692f3504db581ae77c032" :answer/id #uuid "95f4c7fa-d2c2-52cf-95f6-4969d599204d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\,150" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29182/selection/cf8b7eb1c74830b44434c38ded100eb3af6fdb9ad0764d3465f72b510821a1af" :answer/id #uuid "56d8b759-364f-5c68-917b-92dd301ccbe1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-29182/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d6c5a6-7bbd-5313-b64a-22e75ed00152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-29182/selection/83571eb82b4127271b1e2addbddab517c93e99c9300074c00dd941e05abafa15" :answer-field/choices ["q-29182/selection/83571eb82b4127271b1e2addbddab517c93e99c9300074c00dd941e05abafa15" "q-29182/selection/56aa5ec6f972b749f2f5258741a049b6681dc2c06bc4245d9d9e7f9af7d90117" "q-29182/selection/1b07ec847b0ad69ffaa1f16e85ab83d4a29995c786c7293f0c7f63ccc7740310" "q-29182/selection/9bf898dca7fc0a32413abe9b644d58c4852132a28ce692f3504db581ae77c032" "q-29182/selection/cf8b7eb1c74830b44434c38ded100eb3af6fdb9ad0764d3465f72b510821a1af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-29182" :question/id #uuid "23dbd8da-da31-5df7-bb91-fad8d1a7bceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-29182" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={30}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={30}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/8c6e3ae6bdbfb1c66aee719f77225453992fffe6f3c6e3dfdc3f1ff92c59863a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={330}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required dommain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{30}^{∘},-{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-30\\cdot (-330)=9\\,900\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-29182/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-29182"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325125/field-1/8ca4b7a1e89c730869f48c3a3ace0473b36e7c19c70ef8efef65592fedc719dd" :answer/id #uuid "57bb8a21-2552-5dd0-bdf4-ff91c64b6ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325125/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e5aa6f-9a17-54a6-ae93-a67e558f8d79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325125/field-1/8ca4b7a1e89c730869f48c3a3ace0473b36e7c19c70ef8efef65592fedc719dd" :answer-field/choices ["q-325125/field-1/8ca4b7a1e89c730869f48c3a3ace0473b36e7c19c70ef8efef65592fedc719dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325125/field-2/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583" :answer/id #uuid "3de62f0d-cac3-5cfc-9ebf-f32d6295f8e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325125/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a383ec2-309e-5dad-9ff2-55d13cc4f6d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325125/field-2/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583" :answer-field/choices ["q-325125/field-2/ca566c3861ad95266b92d3d8e36c85c39daf1d1780cc1bf826ae0ac7accce583"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325125" :question/id #uuid "71b4b55b-5a45-5f2c-88ad-262e17c40ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-325125" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-45\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=45\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-325125/field-1" "q-325125/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325125"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-29179"] :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={60}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={60}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={300}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{60}^{∘},-{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-60\\cdot (-300)=18\\,000\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-274900/field-1/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708" :answer/id #uuid "0f180271-5813-5675-af96-87a62fd5b4be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274900/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "698f3a74-cd06-56b3-9a8a-5066d31165a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274900/field-1/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708" :answer-field/choices ["q-274900/field-1/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274900/field-2/a4c8bdda186d0d425e9963bf17967cd5e356d934f319fa58135b8f8cf8a589f8" :answer/id #uuid "e10babe8-be76-5d41-a5ba-9f5c9fbb8bf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274900/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9bc9efc-d6b2-5b95-a4ae-5c4495b26294" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274900/field-2/a4c8bdda186d0d425e9963bf17967cd5e356d934f319fa58135b8f8cf8a589f8" :answer-field/choices ["q-274900/field-2/a4c8bdda186d0d425e9963bf17967cd5e356d934f319fa58135b8f8cf8a589f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274900" :question/id #uuid "15a54511-44fe-53d8-abbd-3c1eb65e9175" :question/math-academy-id "q-274900" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/3f3f1422eca9190a5e0827da456315ba88b074f0bf5e9aab1bb325d131bf1984.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-30\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=30\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-274900/field-1" "q-274900/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28062dbf-815b-5221-ae86-1050e106c5ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-274900"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120476/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "3c30cd10-d947-5b5f-a170-626be7bc2a01" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120476/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "779ec274-c728-5729-af3f-5312c455aaa8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120476/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "a22f938f-0fc8-599a-9949-1bb024d374cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120476/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "35be4a37-e374-5ecd-9063-2abeff8aab05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120476/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b1815d22-86d3-532b-ad4f-ce00ea66e49c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120476/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c954d3-ee47-5f53-a702-30dd2088dd72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120476/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-120476/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120476/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-120476/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-120476/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-120476/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120476" :question/id #uuid "129a7963-e7f1-51c5-8a9e-c69dd218f1f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120476" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, equations I, II, and III have no solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-120476/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120476"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76503/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" :answer/id #uuid "2514b3f7-9bbe-5577-a254-e5f4adf577e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76503/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "64844171-b49b-5f4d-b84b-531246e88255" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76503/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "619b1d7b-0ab7-5ac2-8286-5495138c75e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76503/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "78034b41-35c9-5063-8bb1-860f3e356084" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76503/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c1