ollowing equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\n- $4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-7=3+2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+3=1$ $$\n- $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-5=-6$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-120487/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120487"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-29163"] :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-120481/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d312aac3-584a-5fbd-9a37-44e8f9b8e2ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120481/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "fa8c9efd-0d7b-5eba-83ed-3c1ea89774b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120481/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "fb317fac-de29-5bfb-9deb-ad0253191a77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120481/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "1c654d9e-afff-5be7-a40e-455dc8afcf4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120481/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "19cf6bde-55ed-5adb-a639-ba9670f8f3b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120481/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9de5fb98-a523-51c8-aaab-cc7a06ba8d33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120481/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-120481/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-120481/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-120481/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-120481/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-120481/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120481" :question/id #uuid "8dc4e71e-b2cc-535e-8704-1528710848d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120481" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$$$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120481/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120481"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3527"] :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sec x+4=0$ for $0 \\leq x <             2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sec x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x =  \\dfrac{1}{(-2)}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ Since $\\cos{x} < 0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/0e370925faa2a4abc7ac613657ecdf27c2fa0eaa66cff05b077f77a71999c742.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{8\\pi^2}{9}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4260"] :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x <                2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=1.230\\,959…≈1.231\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 1.231\\,0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/50df7e600a9350f2413da4060293116cccf770df200e55f6534d953d68df42ba.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.231\\,0 \\\\  & =5.052\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3529"] :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}$ for $-180^\\circ \\leq  x <            180^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 45^\\circ.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution $x_2$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $360^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_2 = 315^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ,-45^\\circ.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = 45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3532"] :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi?$\n\n- $\\sec x  = -1$\n\n- $\\sec x  = 2$\n\n- $\\sec x  = \\dfrac 1 3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\sec x$ for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\sec x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\sec x = k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
